course: dictionary
lesson: 10146
title: Words that beat
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Throb is a word you will meet in stories and in health talk. It can be a verb or a noun. As a verb, it means to beat strongly and regularly, like a heart, a drum or an engine. When it describes a part of the body, it means a strong pain that comes and goes in beats.
explain: «A hole grew in a back tooth, it began to throb at night.» The clues are at night and the dentist had to take the tooth out. A throbbing tooth is one that hurts in strong, pulsing waves, so throb must mean a painful beat. Check it: swap in hurt and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Throb can also be a noun: a throb is one strong beat, like the throb of an engine. Say it aloud and listen for the beat in the word: throb, throb, throb.
fact: Throb means to beat strongly and regularly, like a heart.
fact: «Throbbing» is the -ing form: «my head was throbbing».
fact: A throbbing pain hurts in beats, and it comes and goes.
fact: As a noun, a throb is one strong, regular beat, as in the throb of an engine.
fact: Useful phrases: throbbing headache, throbbing pain, heart throbbed.
exercise: «The boy's tooth began to throb at night.» What does throb mean here? | *To hurt with a strong pain that comes and goes in beats | To shine very brightly | To sing a happy tune | To be completely silent
exercise: Which sentence uses throb to mean a strong, regular beat? | The dog began to sleep under the table. | The window began to crack in the cold. | *The drum began to throb loudly through the hall. | The cake began to rise slowly in the oven.
exercise: «The engine gave a steady throb as the boat left the harbour.» What is the noun in this sentence? | boat | steady | *throb | engine
test:
«My thumb is throbbing after I hit it with the hammer.» What does throbbing show? | *The thumb hurts with a strong pain that comes and goes in beats | The thumb is clean and dry | The thumb is made of wood | The thumb is bending backwards
A child says throb only means a heart beating. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean a painful beat, or the beat of an engine | Yes, only hearts can throb | Yes, but only in music | No, it means to fall asleep
exam:
What made the Texas boy's tooth so painful that the dentist had to take it out? | *He had hardly brushed for a year, so a hole grew in the tooth | He ate too many apples and the tooth went shiny | He sang too loudly and the tooth got tired | He wore a bright hat and the tooth went red
